      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nSTEPHEN C. GORDON, ESQ., Assistant Federal Public Defender, Raleigh, North Carolina; on behalf of Petitioner.\nCURTIS E. GANNON, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2010-10-5258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "20bcb13ee8d83269368ea9ab9f5d24cae285bf2b6ebb8bb3a40a780dbccefe1c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-20bcb13ee8d8-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When this Court construes a statute, the words of the statute matter, the purpose of the statute matters, and the results produced by that construction matter."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2010-10-5258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "20bcb13ee8d83269368ea9ab9f5d24cae285bf2b6ebb8bb3a40a780dbccefe1c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-20bcb13ee8d8-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When Congress defined in the Armed Career Criminal Act a \"serious\" drug offense as one for which a State penalty of 10 years or more is prescribed by law, it meant for Federal courts to look to the law presently in effect in that State."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-5258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "20bcb13ee8d83269368ea9ab9f5d24cae285bf2b6ebb8bb3a40a780dbccefe1c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-20bcb13ee8d8-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What is the maximum punishment for that offense which occurred at that time is the longer period. Why is -- I don't see why your argument requires us to ask what would be the term if he had committed the offense at a later date."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-10-5258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "20bcb13ee8d83269368ea9ab9f5d24cae285bf2b6ebb8bb3a40a780dbccefe1c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-20bcb13ee8d8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Could I -- just to clarify your point. If there's been a modification of law that would have entitled him to a retroactive change in his sentence, although I'm not quite sure how that works, because if there's a modification, I thought it would only apply to a defendant who had committed the crime at that earlier time but was convicted at the present time. This defendant wouldn't have his sentence modified."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen C. Gordon (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2010-10-5258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "20bcb13ee8d83269368ea9ab9f5d24cae285bf2b6ebb8bb3a40a780dbccefe1c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-20bcb13ee8d8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "REBUTTAL ARGUMENT OF STEPHEN C. GORDON ON BEHALF OF THE PETITIONER MR. GORDON: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. First, the government talks about, again, the problems arising if -- if a statute is amended, and so forth. Again, I would like to analogize that to the violent felony prong of ACCA. Predicate convictions disappear. Things that were predicate convictions cease to be when this Court interprets a particular offense as not qualifying as a violent felony, then -- then individuals who had committed that offense it disappears as -- as a predicate. So it is -- it is not anomalous for that to happen."
    },
